            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Boston to Iceland?</h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $350 to $650 round-trip. For summer visits, book 45 to 70 days ahead and target April or September. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing throughout the year.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Boston to Iceland?</h3>
            <p>Icelandair operates daily year-round nonstop flights from Boston to Keflavík. PLAY offers low-cost nonstop service. Delta provides seasonal summer nonstops. Flight duration is approximately five and a half hours, landing on the Reykjanes Peninsula near Reykjavík.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Iceland from the US?</h3>
            <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in Iceland. You may stay up to 90 days within the Schengen Area. A valid passport is required. Iceland participates in Schengen border rules, so time counts toward your total European allowance.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Boston to Iceland flight?</h3>
            <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds, one carry-on, and a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. Icelandair includes baggage on standard fares. PLAY charges separately for checked bags. Always verify your specific airline's policy before packing.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Keflavík Airport to Reykjavík?</h3>
            <p>The Flybus express coach costs approximately ISK 3,500 and reaches Reykjavík in 45 minutes. Airport Direct offers similar service. Taxis and private transfers run ISK 15,000 to 20,000. Rental cars are essential for countryside exploration beyond the capital.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Iceland, or do I need Icelandic króna?</h3>
            <p>Iceland uses the króna exclusively, though US dollars are rarely accepted outside airports. ATMs are abundant throughout Reykjavík, and credit cards work at virtually all establishments. Contactless payment is universal. Carry minimal cash for remote rural purchases only.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Iceland safe for American tourists?</h3>
            <p>Iceland is among the world's safest countries for visitors. Crime is extremely rare. Natural hazards require more attention than personal safety; sudden weather changes, rough Atlantic surf, and geothermal hot springs pose real risks. Always follow local guidance and warning signs.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Reykjavík for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>The 101 district places you near Laugavegur shopping, Hallgrímskirkja, and nightlife. The Old Harbor suits whale watching and maritime dining. Hlemmur offers local food halls and vintage charm. Grandi provides waterfront design hotels near the harbor and concert hall.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Boston Logan for my Iceland flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from BOS Terminal E. During peak summer travel or holiday weekends, consider arriving three and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Iceland is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays under EU regulations for flights departing Keflavík, and most extend comparable protections for flights to Iceland. Contact your airline immediately and maintain comprehensive travel insurance coverage for maximum protection.</p>




















